<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drunken Sailor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">sung by the 1830s; first described in print from a whaling voyage of <a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Drunken Sailor&quot;, also known as &quot;What Shall We Do with a Drunken Sailor?&quot; or &quot;Up She Rises&quot;, is a traditional sea shanty, number 322 in the Roud Folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Index. Who made it and where is not known. It resembles the traditional Irish song &quot;Óró sé do bheatha abhaile&quot; in its chord progression and in its repeated words over melodic sequences, and it is in the Dorian mode.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first published description of the shanty comes from an account of a whaling voyage of 1839 out of New London, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, to the Pacific, which gave it as a song performed with very good effect when a long line of men hauled together, with the words Ho! Ho! and up she rises, early in the morning. There are hints that it is older. In Minstrelsy of Maine (1927) the editors Eckstorm and Smyth recorded that a grandmother who sang it claimed to have heard it used for tacking on the Penobscot River, by their reckoning well over a hundred years earlier.</p><p>References remain rare. A five-verse text and tune appeared in the third edition of Davis and Tozer's Sailor Songs or 'Chanties', but the song is missing from the other major shanty collections of the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The shanty accompanied tasks done at a brisk walking pace, and it probably belonged to the time when crews, especially on warships, were large enough to haul a rope while simply marching along the deck. John Masefield, publishing it in 1906, called it a runaway chorus, seldom used; others called it a stamp and go or walk away shanty, sung in quick time for tacking. Captain W. B. Whall, an English sailor of the 1860s and 1870s, said it was one of only two shanties sung in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, where singing at work was frowned upon, and that it fell out of use as crews shrank on merchant packet and clipper ships.</p><p>Later sailors remembered other uses. Richard Maitland, an American sailor of the 1870s, told the collector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-lomax">Alan Lomax</a> in 1939 that it was sung while scrubbing the barnacles from an iron ship's bottom, and Frederick Pease Harlow wrote that it served for hauling halyards hand over hand.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>By the 1900s the song had passed ashore. It was sung in glee singing at Eton College, at gatherings of the Savage Club of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> by the 1910s, and as a dance tune in the American Midwest, where a catalogue of folk-songs listed it in 1915. Percy Grainger recorded Charles Rosher of London singing it in 1906 and used it in his Scotch Strathspey And Reel (1924); Malcolm Arnold drew on its melody in his Three Shanties for Woodwind Quintet (1943). The shanty revival from the 1920s made it one of the best-known songs of the repertoire. The Irish Rovers have closed their shows with it for over 50 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Drunken Sailor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
